---
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE LATHE — flagship case study.
//
// A scroll-driven film in six acts. We follow one anonymous file —
// Audio 14.wav — from a lost glint in the chaos to a named, crowned,
// published master. The system is what does the transforming.
//
//   0 · Cold open      — the title card (AtlasHero)
//   1 · Chaos          — make the mess felt (ChaosToBuckets)
//   2 · The sweep      — one organizing motion into five bins
//   3 · The companion  — zoom to one track; Richard appears (RichardReveal)
//   4 · The brain      — taste → 6 variants → the rendition toggle → the loops
//   5 · Resolution     — what it solves, the scale, the meta-twist
//
// Supersedes the prose case study in place (route /case-studies/the-console,
// wired to the homepage card + cover). Backup: the-console.astro.bak-pre-atlas-*.
// Honors prefers-reduced-motion; all motion is IntersectionObserver-gated.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
import CSLayout from "../../components/case-study/CSLayout.astro";
import CSSection from "../../components/case-study/CSSection.astro";
import CSPullquote from "../../components/case-study/CSPullquote.astro";
import CSLiveDemo from "../../components/case-study/CSLiveDemo.astro";
import AtlasHero from "../../components/case-study/atlas/AtlasHero.astro";
import ChaosToBuckets from "../../components/case-study/atlas/ChaosToBuckets.astro";
import FileTransform from "../../components/case-study/atlas/FileTransform.astro";
import RichardReveal from "../../components/case-study/atlas/RichardReveal.astro";
import EngineerBrain from "../../components/case-study/atlas/EngineerBrain.astro";
import RenditionToggle from "../../components/case-study/atlas/RenditionToggle.astro";
import TasteLoops from "../../components/case-study/atlas/TasteLoops.astro";
import Movement from "../../components/case-study/atlas/Movement.astro";
import LoudnessBias from "../../components/case-study/atlas/LoudnessBias.astro";
import ClosingDuo from "../../components/case-study/atlas/ClosingDuo.astro";
import SecretDoor from "../../components/case-study/atlas/SecretDoor.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<CSLayout
  title="The Lathe — Jon Tollefson"
  description="The Lathe — eight years of scattered sound, swept into one place that knows what it has and hands it back to you, smarter. A live, interactive case study with a mix engineer that learns your taste."
  ogImage="/case-studies/the-console/cover.jpg"
>
  <AtlasHero
    eyebrow="Case study 01 · An AI that finishes my music — without ever making it"
    title="The Lathe"
    subtitle="Eight years of scattered sound. One place that knows what it has — and hands it back to you, smarter."
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Designer, builder, sole engineer" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "May 2026 — present" },
      { label: "Stack", value: "Astro · FastAPI · Python · Pedalboard · Whisper · Claude" },
      { label: "Scale", value: "20,279 files · 5 tiers · 6 mixes a track" },
    ]}
    glossary={{
      term: "lathe",
      ipa: "/leɪð/",
      pos: "noun",
      senses: [
        {
          tag: "the cutting lathe",
          text: "The machine that writes sound into matter. A heated sapphire rides the master and carves it live, one pass, no undo, as a single unbroken spiral valley in lacquer. Every swell, every transient, every held breath transcribed into a groove a needle can later re-sing. The last physical act of mastering: the moment a mix stops being a file and becomes a thing you can hold to the light.",
        },
        {
          tag: "the workshop root",
          text: "The oldest machine on the bench. It spins a rough piece against one fixed edge and turns it down to a true, symmetrical form. Chaos in, shape out.",
        },
      ],
      etymology: "Middle English, of Scandinavian root: a frame that holds the work, and turns it true.",
    }}
  />

  {/* ─────── 01 · THE WISH / THE MESS ─────── */}
  <CSSection
    num="01"
    title="Sit down to make something"
    deck="The blank session waits. You half-remember a vocal you sang into your phone last Tuesday. You open Finder. iCloud hasn't downloaded the file yet. By the time it shows up, the idea is gone."
    noTopRule
  >
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>That moment — repeated a thousand times across eight years of writing music — is the gap this whole thing closes. Twenty thousand audio files: every voice memo, every jam, every bounce, every sample I've ever touched, scattered across four drives and iCloud and my desktop and friends' DMs. None of it findable. <span class="k-pink">Most of it never heard again.</span></p>
      <p>So I built a machine to <span class="k-cyan">sweep it all into one place</span>. Scroll.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 1 + 2 · CHAOS → THE SWEEP (theater) ─────── */}
  <ChaosToBuckets />

  {/* ═══════ MOVEMENT I · AUDIODEX (the index) ═══════ */}
  <Movement
    index="I"
    kicker="the index"
    name="AudioDex"
    tagline="It reads, transcribes, and ranks twenty thousand of my own scattered files — so for the first time I know what I have, and what's worth finishing."
    accent="cyan"
    mark="audiodex"
    flush
  />

  {/* ─────── 02 · THE CATALOG KNOWS WHAT IT HAS ─────── */}
  <CSSection
    num="02"
    title="It fills in its own entries"
    deck="Before AudioDex, every one of these was a dead name in a folder. Three voice memos, each filed by iOS under nothing but a date and a number. Pick one and run the lathe: it reads the whole thing and cuts its own entry."
  >
    <div class="cs-bleed"><div class="ft-frame"><FileTransform /></div></div>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>That's <em>one</em> entry — the hardest kind. The other twenty thousand got the same treatment, most of them with no help from me. An auto-classifier reads each row's content, source, and length and files it: <strong>Jams</strong> and <strong>Field</strong> sort themselves — the first run filed 1,389 field rows and 329 jams untouched — while <strong>Loops</strong> and <strong>Tracks</strong> stay curator-picked and <strong>Inbox</strong> holds the residue.</p>
      <p>That's the move that changes everything: Whisper transcribes the talking and the singing, Claude reads the transcript and <span class="k-cyan">names the file and sums it up</span>, and librosa stamps BPM and key where there's music to find. A date-and-a-number becomes a title, a sentence, and a searchable transcript — and the pile of filenames becomes a thing that knows what it's holding.</p>
      <p>This is the opposite of a sample manager. Tools like Algonaut&nbsp;Atlas or Waves&nbsp;Cosmos organize clean one-shots you already chose to keep, by how they sound — <em>find me a kick</em>. <span class="adx">AudioDex</span> starts from the other end of the mess: twenty thousand unnamed, half-finished, unevaluated files, and the one question that actually blocks me — <strong>what do I even have, and what's worth finishing?</strong></p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ═══════ MOVEMENT II · RICHARD (the engineer) ═══════ */}
  <Movement
    index="II"
    kicker="the engineer"
    name="Richard"
    tagline="A mix engineer that masters every track six ways, learns which one you crown, and asks when it's unsure."
    accent="green"
    mark="richard"
  />

  {/* ─────── ACT 3 · THE COMPANION ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="03" title="Send it over" deck="Zoom all the way in on one row in Tracks: a finished song, mixed rough, on headphones, at 2 a.m. The next step was always the lonely one — master it, A/B it against records you love, decide if it's done. So I built someone to do it with.">
    <RichardReveal />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 4a · THE TASTE ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="04" title="Taste, built from the records I love" deck="Richard 🐢 isn't a preset — he's a guitar-slinging turtle with opinions. His taste is built from a corpus of finished records I love, each one tagged with what actually works in it. Those notes anchor three axes, and every job masters the track six ways across them.">
    <CSLiveDemo label="LIVE · TAP A RECORD" caption="The real reference catalog — loved records, each with a one-line note on what I like in it. Tap one to see which taste pole it anchors. matchering matches the track's overall tonal balance, loudness, and stereo width toward that reference, then holds a −1 dBTP ceiling — it shapes the whole mix's character, it never isolates or moves a part. Six variants come back per song." bleed pad="comfy">
      <EngineerBrain />
    </CSLiveDemo>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 4b · THE PROBLEM: LOUDNESS BIAS ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="05" title="The catch: louder always wins" deck="Before you can tell which master is better, you hit the trap every engineer knows and most listeners don't. Two takes — press 1 · 2, trust your gut, hold to vote.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE"
      caption="An honest A/B has to be loudness-matched first. Otherwise a hair more level reads as fuller lows and crisper highs — it's how our ears work, the equal-loudness curves — and the louder one wins on volume alone. Doing that by hand, for six masters, is the tax no one sees: print each, meter its LUFS, trim the gain a hair, re-check. Trust your gut above; the demo shows you why this step isn't optional."
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <LoudnessBias />
    </CSLiveDemo>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 4c · THE RENDITION TOGGLE (centerpiece) ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="06" title="The same passage, three ways" deck="So the comparison has to be blind and loudness-matched — the grind you just felt, done for you. “head to toe,” a real track from my catalog: three masters Richard 🐢 rendered across his taste axes — bright, warm, and open — all matched to the same loudness. Press 1 · 2 · 3 — the finishing changes underneath you, the playhead never loses its place.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE"
      caption="Real masters, straight off the Engine — sixteen bars of “head to toe,” three ways, loudness-matched so you're judging the finishing, not the volume (the bright one came back a full 3 LU hotter). Blind by default — hit reveal to see which is which and the record each was matched toward; hold a key to crown one. This is the gesture the whole Engineer is built around."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <RenditionToggle />
    </CSLiveDemo>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>In the real app it's a blind <strong>A / B / original</strong> — three masters and the untouched <span class="k-pink">original</span> (it only turns up red <em>after</em> you vote), all shuffled and loudness-matched. No pole names, no “this one's the master” tell — you just hold a key to vote, and the engine is the only one keeping score. Three axes judged, three winners, consolidated into one card per song. <strong>That</strong> blind test is what produced the number in the next section.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 4c · THE LOOPS ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="07" title="It learns. Then it asks.">
    <TasteLoops />
    <div class="prose-block" style="margin-top: 30px;">
      <p>Under the hood, the engine scans its own vote history for what surprises it — an original that beat every master, a QA-flagged take I crowned anyway, the same complaint three times — and turns the most surprising one into the question. My answer never retrains the model wholesale; it's a small, <span class="k-cyan">bounded nudge</span> on which references it reaches for next, scoped to songs that sound like this one. The taste loop runs both directions now.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 4d · HOW IT STANDS OUT ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="08" title="Why this isn't just another mastering button" deck="There's no shortage of AI that will master a track. There's nothing that masters yours to your taste, inside your own library.">
    <div class="standout">
      <div class="so-row so-them">
        <span class="so-tag">Everyone else</span>
        <p>LANDR, Ozone, the rest are a <strong>stateless upload box</strong>: send a stereo file, get a master back, it forgets you. None reference the records <em>you</em> love. None learn your ear. None live in your library.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="so-row so-this">
        <span class="so-tag">The Lathe</span>
        <p>References the records I actually love, A/Bs them <strong>blind and loudness-matched</strong> — the method engineers insist on — and sharpens its read on my taste with every vote. The catalog and the taste model <strong>compound</strong>: the more I use it, the more it's mine.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="so-foot">The comparison method is the textbook-correct one, and the open engine underneath placed <strong>ahead of every commercial AI mastering service</strong> in a 472-person blind test. The point was never to beat a human engineer on a label release — it's to make eight years of my own work finishable, in my own ear.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── RESOLUTION ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="09" title="Audio 14 has a name now" deck="It got crowned, and it went out. The distance between “this loop is interesting” and “this song is done” used to be a dozen apps and most of a weekend. Now it's a few rooms in one house, and the machine never chose a note for me.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p class="thesis-line"><span class="adx">AudioDex</span> to know what I have. <span class="rich">Richard</span> 🐢 to hand it back smarter. Together, <span class="k-orange">the Lathe</span>.</p>
      <p><strong>Restraint was the design.</strong> One rule held the whole build together: <span class="k-orange">utility, not synthesis</span>. A soundbender chooses the sound; the machine only ever carries it. The calls I'm proudest of are the ones I refused to build.</p>
    </div>

    <ul class="wont">
      <li>It won't write my posts or pitch me song ideas.</li>
      <li>It won't push prompts or listen in on a session.</li>
      <li>It won't publish to streaming, or touch the desktop.</li>
      <li>It won't make one creative decision for me.</li>
    </ul>

    <CSPullquote hero cite="The Lathe">
      Utility, not synthesis. The machine sorts, fingerprints, surfaces, and clears the friction. The artist still chooses, still drags, still arranges.
    </CSPullquote>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── CODA · what it gave me (ear-training) ─────── */}
  <section class="cs-section cs-rule">
    <div class="cs-inner"><ClosingDuo /></div>
  </section>

  {/* ─────── THE THRESHOLD · the seam in the wall (speak "soundbender") ─────── */}
  <SecretDoor />

  <div class="cs-foot">
    <a href="/#work" class="cs-foot-back">← back to selected work</a>
  </div>
</CSLayout>

<style>
  .prose-block { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block em { font-style: italic; color: var(--cs-text); }

  /* inline accent helpers — used sparingly to color key phrases and to track the
     two halves the way the Movement pillars do (AudioDex = cyan, Richard = green) */
  .adx { color: var(--brand-cyan); font-weight: 600; }
  .rich { color: var(--brand-green); font-weight: 600; }
  .k-cyan { color: var(--brand-cyan); }
  .k-green { color: var(--brand-green); }
  .k-pink { color: var(--brand-pink); }
  .k-orange { color: var(--brand-orange); }
  .thesis-line { font-size: 16px; color: var(--cs-text); }

  /* the file-transform beat: full-bleed canvas, capped + centered reading width
     (longhand margins so the .cs-bleed margin-left trick on the parent survives) */
  .ft-frame {
    max-width: 1240px;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
    margin-top: 30px;
    margin-bottom: 44px;
    padding: 0 32px;
  }

  .wont {
    list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 26px 0 8px; max-width: 64ch;
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 6px 22px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 720px) { .wont { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .wont li {
    position: relative; padding-left: 22px;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 13px; color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.78); line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wont li::before { content: "·"; position: absolute; left: 6px; color: var(--brand-pink); font-weight: 700; }

  .standout { max-width: 72ch; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; }
  .standout p { margin: 0; max-width: none; }
  .so-row { border-radius: 8px; padding: 15px 18px 15px 20px; }
  .so-row p { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; }
  .so-tag { display: inline-block; font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace; font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: 0.2em; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .so-them { border: 1px solid var(--cs-line); border-left: 3px solid rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.3); background: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.02); }
  .so-them .so-tag { color: var(--cs-text-dim); }
  .so-them p { color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.72); }
  .so-this { border: 1px solid rgba(0, 255, 77, 0.22); border-left: 3px solid var(--brand-green); background: rgba(0, 255, 77, 0.04); }
  .so-this .so-tag { color: var(--brand-green); }
  .so-foot { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--cs-text-muted); margin-top: 6px; }
  .so-foot em { color: #f5f1e8; font-style: italic; }

  .cs-foot { text-align: center; padding: 64px 0 0; }
  .cs-foot-back {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.32);
    border-radius: 2px;
    transition: color 160ms ease, border-color 160ms ease, background 160ms ease;
  }
  .cs-foot-back:hover {
    color: #f5f1e8;
    border-color: var(--brand-orange);
    background: rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.08);
  }
</style>
